1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:00:04,292
Cowork OS, Scaling Agent Coworkers for Real World Automation.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:17,508
The Cowork OS team introduces a three-layer agent harness that separates task strategy from execution management, successfully allowing agents to handle both complex productivity tasks and long horizon monitoring.

3
00:00:18,126 --> 00:00:32,067
The system proved highly competitive across 12 benchmarks against frontier frameworks like open hands and deep seek harness, and successfully drove a five-round recursive self-improvement process that boosted a QN model score by nearly 10 points.

4
00:00:32,974 --> 00:00:38,564
Welcome to AI Paper Slop, where we break down the latest research for AI engineers and researchers.

5
00:00:40,206 --> 00:00:49,680
To understand why a new operating system for agents was built, we first need to look at how the role of AI agents has shifted from simple workflows to sustained productivity.

6
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:50,800
Yeah, exactly.

7
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:59,280
And the paper actually maps out this evolution through three distinct stages, which really helps frame the exact problem CoworkOS is trying to solve.

8
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:03,040
So stage one was what they called the agent workflow.

9
00:01:03,246 --> 00:01:09,600
Like if you remember the early days of integrating language models, this was all about reasoning and tool using trajectories.

10
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:10,560
Oh, sure.

11
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:12,960
Like interleaved reasoning and action, right?

12
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:15,200
Like React or DSPY.

13
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:16,480
Yes, exactly.

14
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:24,320
The entire goal there was just getting the model to execute a reliable linear sequence of steps to basically arrive at a single answer.

15
00:01:24,814 --> 00:01:28,000
Which, I mean, that feels like ancient history now, but it was a big deal.

16
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:28,880
It was.

17
00:01:29,262 --> 00:01:34,240
But then we moved into stage two, which the authors classify as the agent assistant.

18
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:34,720
Okay.

19
00:01:35,022 --> 00:01:35,920
So what changed there?

20
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:42,560
Well, this is where you introduce persistent memory, messaging and tool interfaces into a continuing interaction.

21
00:01:42,894 --> 00:01:46,720
Frameworks like autogen or langchain really embody this era.

22
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:49,680
Right, because you aren't just firing off a single prompt anymore.

23
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:56,320
You have an assistant that can actually recall previous messages in the session and you know use external tools to fetch data.

24
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:57,252
Precisely.

25
00:01:57,870 --> 00:02:02,564
But the paper argues we are now entering stage three, which they call agent cowork.

26
00:02:03,214 --> 00:02:06,400
And agent cowork is just a massive shift in scope.

27
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:07,120
How so?

28
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:13,348
We are moving away from isolated automation and towards sustained interactive professional work.

29
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:18,320
Humans and agents are now sharing initiative on evolving artifacts over time.

30
00:02:18,734 --> 00:02:22,948
So like systems like Cloud Code, Codex, and uh deep sea carnus?

31
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:24,080
Exactly.

32
00:02:24,334 --> 00:02:31,440
The agent exists in a shared environment, adjusting its plans as the actual work develops, sometimes over days or even weeks.

33
00:02:31,982 --> 00:02:36,160
But I mean, if you've ever tried to leave an agent running a complex coding task over the weekend.

34
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:37,200
Oh, it's terrifying.

35
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:37,680
Right.

36
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:41,920
You know the absolute dread of coming back to a hallucinated mess on Monday morning.

37
00:02:42,318 --> 00:02:42,960
Just total chaos.

38
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:43,360
Yeah.

39
00:02:43,758 --> 00:02:48,964
And the paper points out that this expansion into sustained productivity reveals a major bottleneck.

40
00:02:49,518 --> 00:02:53,760
Cowork agents face two complementary but highly conflicting requirements.

41
00:02:54,160 --> 00:02:55,440
Which is really the core of the issue.

42
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:56,080
Right.

43
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:59,300
So first, they have to execute complex productivity tasks.

44
00:03:00,014 --> 00:03:05,572
This demands heavy reasoning and tool use to actually write the software or you know draft the CAD designs.

45
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:06,180
Yes.

46
00:03:06,766 --> 00:03:10,692
But second, they have to maintain continued monitoring and decision making.

47
00:03:11,118 --> 00:03:15,200
They have to watch external information and keep their context healthy over long horizons.

48
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:17,828
And doing both is incredibly hard.

49
00:03:18,318 --> 00:03:22,084
Complex delivery basically challenges the agent's problem-solving depth.

50
00:03:22,862 --> 00:03:29,200
Sustained supervision, on the other hand, challenges its ability to retain an objective and respond to changing conditions.

51
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:30,116
Makes sense.

52
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:37,760
When you try to force both of those responsibilities into a single linear context window, the system inevitably collapses under its own weight.

53
00:03:38,254 --> 00:03:53,120
It's basically the human equivalent of expecting one person to be both the deep-focused individual contributor, like the IC wrestling with a thousand lines of Python, and the attentive project manager who monitors the Slack channels and long-running CICD pipelines all week.

54
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:54,400
That's a great way to put it.

55
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:58,084
Yeah, you just can't do both simultaneously without dropping something.

56
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:03,040
So why can't our current agent frameworks handle both at the same time?

57
00:04:03,662 --> 00:04:07,920
The mechanical reason for this in language models is context bloat.

58
00:04:08,142 --> 00:04:18,240
If the agent acts as that deep focus I see, its active context window fills up with, well, minute reasoning steps, failed code executions, raw tool outputs.

59
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:19,440
Just absolute noise.

60
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:20,000
Exactly.

61
00:04:20,173 --> 00:04:27,680
So when it tries to switch back to the project manager role to evaluate the global objective, it has literally lost the forest for the trees.

62
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:32,800
The original objective is buried under thousands of tokens of intermediate trial and error.

63
00:04:33,280 --> 00:04:37,840
Which leads directly to hallucination or task deviation.

64
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:38,800
Precisely.

65
00:04:39,376 --> 00:04:41,220
So the architecture makes sense in theory.

66
00:04:41,456 --> 00:04:43,856
You need to separate the manager from the worker.

67
00:04:44,206 --> 00:04:54,372
And Cowork OS attempts to solve this bottleneck by introducing a three layer hierarchical architecture that explicitly separates authority from execution.

68
00:04:54,766 --> 00:04:58,496
Let's break down the mechanics of these layers, starting at the top with the main agent.

69
00:04:59,056 --> 00:04:59,296
Sure.

70
00:04:59,376 --> 00:05:02,736
So the main agent is the system's primary point of accountability.

71
00:05:02,926 --> 00:05:08,416
It faces the user, it holds the global task context, and it basically acts as the overall planner.

72
00:05:08,896 --> 00:05:10,250
So it doesn't do the grunt work?

73
00:05:10,736 --> 00:05:14,500
Well, it is responsible for maintaining the ultimate objective of the workflow.

74
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:24,256
It actually does have the ability to act directly when a task absolutely requires global context, but its primary function in complex scenarios is to delegate.

75
00:05:24,718 --> 00:05:28,132
And it delegates down to the bottom layer of this architecture, which is the subagents.

76
00:05:28,416 --> 00:05:28,656
Right.

77
00:05:29,056 --> 00:05:31,376
And the paper notes something really interesting here.

78
00:05:31,536 --> 00:05:35,396
These subagents do not receive the entire conversational history.

79
00:05:35,726 --> 00:05:43,044
They receive a bounded background, just the context they need for their specific assignment, along with a compact catalog of skills and tools.

80
00:05:43,536 --> 00:05:45,536
Yes, and that boundary is critical.

81
00:05:45,902 --> 00:05:54,256
They execute their scoped assignments, maybe running parallel investigations, and then return what the paper calls a report and evidence to the main agent.

82
00:05:54,894 --> 00:05:58,096
But they deliberately do not return their internal reasoning trajectories.

83
00:05:58,416 --> 00:05:58,896
No, they don't.

84
00:05:59,566 --> 00:06:03,936
The authors refer to this dynamic as delegation as an evidence contract.

85
00:06:04,416 --> 00:06:05,696
An evidence contract.

86
00:06:06,096 --> 00:06:06,256
Yeah.

87
00:06:06,336 --> 00:06:13,476
So the main agent specifies the objectives, the constraints, and the exact form of evidence it expects to see at the boundary of the assignment.

88
00:06:14,062 --> 00:06:20,096
The subagent navigates the messy, token heavy process of trial and error, but only brings back the clean, verifiable result.

89
00:06:20,558 --> 00:06:22,976
So it's kind of like a sous-chef in a commercial kitchen.

90
00:06:23,246 --> 00:06:27,936
The head chef doesn't want to know how many times you dropped the knife or, you know, the internal monologue you had while chopping the onions.

91
00:06:28,256 --> 00:06:28,816
They really don't.

92
00:06:29,230 --> 00:06:32,496
They just want the perfectly diced onions on the pass, ready for the dish.

93
00:06:32,910 --> 00:06:35,296
That keeps the head chef's mental context perfectly clean.

94
00:06:35,470 --> 00:06:35,656
Aaron Powell

95
00:06:35,656 --> 00:06:36,176
Exactly.

96
00:06:36,654 --> 00:06:46,496
And it keeps the main agent's context window pristine, preserving its ability to manage the long horizon objective without getting bogged down by the sub-agent's execution noise.

97
00:06:46,896 --> 00:06:49,456
I have to push back on the structural reality of this, though.

98
00:06:50,318 --> 00:06:50,816
Lay it on me.

99
00:06:51,136 --> 00:07:00,932
In standard software engineering, adding a middleman runtime layer between a manager and a worker often introduces severe latency configuration nightmares or state sync issues.

100
00:07:01,326 --> 00:07:04,736
And we skipped over the middle layer of this architecture, the coordinator runtime.

101
00:07:05,136 --> 00:07:05,536
We did, yeah.

102
00:07:05,934 --> 00:07:11,136
So what makes this coordinator runtime actually useful for the model rather than just being overhead?

103
00:07:11,456 --> 00:07:12,644
That's a very fair question.

104
00:07:12,896 --> 00:07:18,736
The coordinator runtime acts as the control plane boundary between the main agent and the subagents.

105
00:07:18,896 --> 00:07:25,296
And it is entirely responsible for managing stable identities, message queues, and persistent program state.

106
00:07:25,696 --> 00:07:27,216
Okay, so it's doing a lot of heavy lifting.

107
00:07:27,616 --> 00:07:28,016
A ton.

108
00:07:28,398 --> 00:07:35,588
Imagine a subagent is assigned a data processing task that takes three days to run and the server temporarily goes down.

109
00:07:35,936 --> 00:07:38,896
The coordinator maintains the durable state of that assignment.

110
00:07:39,086 --> 00:07:42,816
It handles the mechanics of both synchronous and asynchronous execution.

111
00:07:43,246 --> 00:07:54,416
Ah, So if the main agent delegates a task asynchronously, it essentially gets a receipt, like an identifier from the coordinator, and can go monitor something else while the subagent works?

112
00:07:54,896 --> 00:07:56,176
Yes, exactly.

113
00:07:56,416 --> 00:08:03,136
And when the subagent eventually finishes, the coordinator exposes the final report and the evidence to the main agent.

114
00:08:03,566 --> 00:08:04,900
Without merging the contexts.

115
00:08:05,456 --> 00:08:05,696
Right.

116
00:08:06,030 --> 00:08:12,196
Crucially, it moves this evidence across the boundary without merging the full context windows of the two agents.

117
00:08:12,496 --> 00:08:20,976
The coordinator ensures that these exchanges are durable and observable, guaranteeing the survival of the task context across recoverable interruptions.

118
00:08:21,456 --> 00:08:26,016
So it is the functional mechanism that prevents the context bloat we discussed earlier.

119
00:08:26,638 --> 00:08:27,204
Spot on.

120
00:08:27,790 --> 00:08:32,227
Okay, let's pivot and look at how human engineers actually interact with this system.

121
00:08:32,782 --> 00:08:40,511
If the agents are operating in this heavily structured three-layer environment, how do you inject human feedback without completely breaking the agent's workflow?

122
00:08:40,912 --> 00:08:45,152
Yeah, that's where the third section of the Cowork OS architecture comes in.

123
00:08:45,358 --> 00:08:52,672
It details a shared application ecosystem that supports native services for documents, slides, CAD, and Blender.

124
00:08:53,072 --> 00:08:59,364
which means that humans and agents are inspecting and revising the exact same evolving artifacts.

125
00:08:59,854 --> 00:09:05,712
Like if an agent is working on a 3D model in Blender, the human designer is looking at that same Blender file in real time.

126
00:09:06,032 --> 00:09:06,192
Right.

127
00:09:06,352 --> 00:09:07,952
They are literally in the same workspace.

128
00:09:08,494 --> 00:09:10,912
But I mean concurrent editing is notoriously difficult.

129
00:09:11,054 --> 00:09:16,912
If both the human and the agent try to modify the file at the exact same moment, you risk corrupting the state.

130
00:09:17,312 --> 00:09:17,712
You do.

131
00:09:18,032 --> 00:09:23,172
Which is why to prevent this, Cowork OS introduces what they call the resource protocol.

132
00:09:23,630 --> 00:09:29,552
It relies on a specific explicit revision checking mechanism, which is detailed as equation seven in the paper.

133
00:09:29,952 --> 00:09:30,980
Okay, equation seven.

134
00:09:31,632 --> 00:09:32,836
How does that work in practice?

135
00:09:33,390 --> 00:09:36,992
The protocol essentially treats revision history as a first-class citizen.

136
00:09:37,326 --> 00:09:39,232
It functions as a strict gatekeeper.

137
00:09:39,632 --> 00:09:40,548
So it blocks edits.

138
00:09:40,992 --> 00:09:46,272
Well, before any new operation is committed to the file, the protocol checks the base revision number.

139
00:09:46,606 --> 00:09:51,792
An edit is only committed if the baseline revision hasn't advanced since the actor initiated the change.

140
00:09:52,112 --> 00:09:52,592
Oh, I see.

141
00:09:52,912 --> 00:09:53,152
Yeah.

142
00:09:53,358 --> 00:10:03,312
If another actor, whether that is a human engineer or a parallel subagent, has modified the resource in the meantime, the protocol blocks the operation and returns a conflict.

143
00:10:03,712 --> 00:10:08,192
Imagine if Git didn't require you to manually resolve merge conflicts.

144
00:10:08,272 --> 00:10:14,832
Like instead of the system crashing, it just looks at your new commit, treats it as a boundary, and tells the agent that the state has changed.

145
00:10:15,232 --> 00:10:15,632
Exactly.

146
00:10:15,950 --> 00:10:22,192
The agent simply reads the new state, observes the conflict, and updates its internal plan based on the new reality of the file.

147
00:10:22,574 --> 00:10:26,912
This creates a major paradigm shift in how we handle human AI interaction.

148
00:10:27,726 --> 00:10:33,632
Because direct human edits to a CAD file or a shared document are treated directly as task guidance by the agent.

149
00:10:33,952 --> 00:10:34,112
Yes.

150
00:10:34,574 --> 00:10:40,432
So the user doesn't have to write a text prompt saying, agent, please stop what you were doing and move the 3D cube to units to the left.

151
00:10:40,752 --> 00:10:41,152
Not at all.

152
00:10:41,614 --> 00:10:44,580
The human just grabs the cube in the CAD software and drags it.

153
00:10:45,070 --> 00:10:49,188
The agent then observes this newly committed state through its domain tools and adapts.

154
00:10:50,094 --> 00:10:56,992
The human in the loop participation is embedded directly within the autonomous execution without relying on text-based course correction.

155
00:10:57,312 --> 00:10:58,592
It's incredibly fluid.

156
00:10:59,310 --> 00:11:05,872
But you know, an elegant architectural concept and a clever resource protocol only matter if they produce tangible improvements.

157
00:11:06,192 --> 00:11:06,352
Right.

158
00:11:06,606 --> 00:11:12,272
The real question is how Cowork OS performs when subjected to rigorous testing against current state-of-the-art frameworks.

159
00:11:12,752 --> 00:11:14,212
And they really put it through its paces.

160
00:11:14,432 --> 00:11:19,872
The researchers set up an extensive evaluation spanning 12 benchmarks across four specific domains.

161
00:11:20,272 --> 00:11:21,028
Which domains were those?

162
00:11:21,392 --> 00:11:24,324
Tool use, productivity, search, and ML coding.

163
00:11:24,752 --> 00:11:31,172
And they benchmarked Cowork OS against serious baselines, primarily open hands, codecs, and deep seat Carnus.

164
00:11:32,032 --> 00:11:32,912
So how did it do?

165
00:11:33,312 --> 00:11:38,672
Across those 12 benchmarks, Cowork OS ranked in the top two in every single domain average.

166
00:11:39,072 --> 00:11:39,952
Wow, every single one.

167
00:11:40,272 --> 00:11:40,512
Yep.

168
00:11:40,592 --> 00:11:44,992
It achieved the highest averages outright in both productivity and ML coding.

169
00:11:45,166 --> 00:11:50,432
It also won specific, highly complex benchmarks like Skills Bench, Job Bench, and Wide Search.

170
00:11:50,512 --> 00:11:50,712
Aaron Powell

171
00:11:50,712 --> 00:11:51,428
That's impressive.

172
00:11:52,192 --> 00:11:59,872
The paper actually provides several qualitative UI examples that visualize exactly why this architecture outperforms the baselines.

173
00:12:00,352 --> 00:12:03,632
Yes, those examples are really helpful for understanding the practical differences.

174
00:12:04,174 --> 00:12:10,592
Like in the office productivity tasks, there was a scenario called Mochau Caves, which involved generating metric cards.

175
00:12:10,912 --> 00:12:12,164
Oh, right, the layout test.

176
00:12:12,512 --> 00:12:12,672
Yeah.

177
00:12:12,992 --> 00:12:18,468
Codex generated descriptions that completely overflowed, extending way below the metric card borders.

178
00:12:18,912 --> 00:12:26,192
Deep Seek Harness had issues where the quantity and duration text wrapped inappropriately and overlapped into the explanatory text.

179
00:12:26,512 --> 00:12:28,100
But Cowork OS managed it.

180
00:12:28,494 --> 00:12:33,712
Yeah, Cowork OS successfully kept all the metric text neatly constrained inside the card borders.

181
00:12:34,112 --> 00:12:36,644
Which shows a much better grasp of spatial constraints.

182
00:12:37,102 --> 00:12:40,832
We see a similar divergence and logical structuring during the AirLC task.

183
00:12:41,152 --> 00:12:42,192
What was the AirLC task?

184
00:12:42,512 --> 00:12:46,912
It required the agents to generate a relation diagram explaining ecological degradation.

185
00:12:47,342 --> 00:12:52,672
Kovacs failed to grasp the multicausal nature of the prompt and forced a strict sequential linear chain.

186
00:12:53,294 --> 00:12:54,592
So it just went A to B D C.

187
00:12:54,912 --> 00:12:55,312
Exactly.

188
00:12:55,472 --> 00:13:00,432
It drew a single line connecting upstream diversion to reduced inflow and finally to evaporation.

189
00:13:00,832 --> 00:13:01,232
Got it.

190
00:13:01,806 --> 00:13:13,472
CoWork OS, though, analyzed the evidence and correctly branched the causes, mapping both reduced inflow and evaporation into a shared outcome diagram, demonstrating a much firmer grasp of complex logical relationships.

191
00:13:14,032 --> 00:13:18,032
So they also tested the system in a CAD environment on a task called CSA2 attention.

192
00:13:18,830 --> 00:13:24,272
Co-work OS clearly displayed state changes and used provenance colors to track modifications.

193
00:13:24,750 --> 00:13:26,672
The Provenance colors are a really nice touch.

194
00:13:26,992 --> 00:13:27,232
Yeah.

195
00:13:27,552 --> 00:13:31,472
And it intelligently used front-facing labels to connect positions to the main cache.

196
00:13:31,854 --> 00:13:35,300
Well, yeah, well, that ensures the visual workspace remained legible, right?

197
00:13:35,552 --> 00:13:43,172
And then uh Deep Sea Carnice, by contrast, that crowded its labels with a low-angle view that just made the state changes difficult to track.

198
00:13:43,790 --> 00:13:46,752
It's just, you know, that's it's all about how the system handles complexity.

199
00:13:47,152 --> 00:13:52,388
But for the ML engineers listening to this, I want to drill down into the ML coding results.

200
00:13:53,102 --> 00:14:02,870
Why does this specific three layer setup give Co-Work OS such a definitive edge in benchmarks like MLE bench light and mlsbench.

201
00:14:03,552 --> 00:14:08,804
All those specific ML coding benchmarks do not just test the ability to write a quick Python script.

202
00:14:09,390 --> 00:14:15,392
They test the ability to run machine learning experiments, evaluate the resulting metrics, and iteratively adjust the code.

203
00:14:15,872 --> 00:14:17,072
Which takes time and patience.

204
00:14:17,472 --> 00:14:17,956
A lot of time.

205
00:14:18,894 --> 00:14:26,192
The three-layer setup is uniquely suited for this because the coordinator runtime natively manages long-running job logs and execution records.

206
00:14:26,542 --> 00:14:26,912
Oh, I see.

207
00:14:27,182 --> 00:14:38,192
The main agent can track the overarching experiment across multiple sessions and compare results from various metric files, while the SEB agents handle the actual code execution, dependency management, and data processing.

208
00:14:38,592 --> 00:14:45,152
So the system doesn't lose the global plot just because a single training run takes six hours to complete.

209
00:14:45,552 --> 00:14:46,148
Precisely.

210
00:14:46,798 --> 00:14:52,992
Which perfectly sets up the ultimate test presented in the paper, recursive self-improvement or RSI.

211
00:14:53,392 --> 00:14:56,672
The RSI experiment is definitely the centerpiece of the paper.

212
00:14:56,992 --> 00:14:57,232
Right.

213
00:14:57,806 --> 00:15:10,116
To prove that Co-Work OS can handle an ML engineering task requiring both complex delivery and sustained supervision over an extended horizon, the researchers deployed the system on an automated post-training loop.

214
00:15:10,592 --> 00:15:18,432
Yes, and the setup for this RSI experiment utilized GLM 5.3 as the backbone model powering the Cowork OS system.

215
00:15:18,592 --> 00:15:18,752
Okay.

216
00:15:18,992 --> 00:15:28,592
The task assigned to Cowork OS was to conduct automated post-training on a distinct base model, specifically QN 3.6-35B-A3B.

217
00:15:28,912 --> 00:15:31,552
And the entire loop was automated by the agent harness.

218
00:15:31,886 --> 00:15:43,460
The process involved analyzing the base model's weaknesses, optimizing the data pipelines, synthesizing new training data, executing the training runs, and then evaluating the results to start the iteration all over again.

219
00:15:43,982 --> 00:15:45,952
It's a huge undertaking for an agent.

220
00:15:46,414 --> 00:15:51,172
And they ran this continuous loop over five full rounds, labeled round zero to round five.

221
00:15:51,758 --> 00:15:59,952
The Quen model's average score across eight distinct benchmarks rose from an initial 33.19 to 42.95.

222
00:16:00,590 --> 00:16:07,232
